Sorting
--------
-> Sorting means arranging data in a specific order
-> Ascending (small → large)
-> Descending (large → small)

[ Visual Representation: Unsorted List [B, D, A, C] ───&gt; Sorted List [A, B, C, D] ]

Sorting algorithms are used in many places like:
------------------------------------------------
1. In Schools: Sorting helps organize student names alphabetically for easy attendance
tracking.
2. eCommerce Sites: Sort algorithms arrange products by price, popularity, or rating to help
customers find what they need quickly.
3. Libraries: Books are sorted by title or author name to make it easy to locate a specific book.
4. Databases: Sorting data in databases makes searching for information faster and more
efficient.
5. Sports: Sorting scores or times helps rank athletes from best to worst.
6. Finance: Sorting stock prices or transaction records helps in analyzing financial data.

Different types of sorting Techniques available Based on comparison
--------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Bubble sort : Compare adjacent elements
2. Selection sort : Find min and max values then place in correct place
3. Insertion sort : Build a &quot;sorted&quot; section one element at a time by inserting the next item into
   its correct position
4. Merge sort : Split the list into half part until they are single elements, then merge them back
   together in order
5. Quick sort : Pick a &quot;pivot&quot; element and move everything smaller to its left and everything
   larger to its right.
6. Heap sort : Convert the list into a &quot;Heap&quot; (a special tree structure) to easily find and &quot;pop&quot;
   the largest value.

1. Bubble Sort:
----------------
- It compares adjacent elements, and swaps them if they are in the wrong order.
  The process is repeated until the list is sorted.
Ex1:-
Consider the list [4, 2, 7, 1].
First pass: Compare 4 and 2, swap to get [2, 4, 7, 1]
Compare 4 and 7, no swap
Compare 7 and 1, swap to get [2, 4, 1, 7]
Second pass: Compare 2 and 4, no swap
Compare 4 and 1, swap to get [2, 1, 4, 7]
Third pass: Compare 2 and 1, swap to get [1, 2, 4, 7]
Fourth pass: List is already sorted, no swaps needed
Final sorted list: [1, 2, 4, 7].

